  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Friday: The offshore low will spiral down the \ncoast, ejecting shortwaves across the Inland NW before it too \npivots inland tonight into Friday. One of those shortwave began \nits trek inland last night and will be moving tracking northeast \nthis morning. Rain along and ahead of it will impact the northeast\nhalf of WA and the ID Panhandle early this morning, shifting to \nmainly the northern counties and Idaho Panhandle late this \nmorning. Thereafter the precipitation start to turn more isolated \nto scattered in nature, becoming more convective focused around \nthe mountains and up against the Cascades for this afternoon. Some\nembedded t-storms are possible this morning along the axis of \nthat shortwave and then in more isolated fashion around the \nmountains this afternoon. Heading into this evening, tonight and \nFriday that low pivots in. The showers and t-storm threat near the\nCascades wane through dark, but farther east shortwave moves up \nthe northern Rockies. That will renew rain chances over southeast \nWA/lower ID this evening and across northeast WA to northern ID \nlate this evening into the overnight, wrapping back toward the \nnorther mountains through Friday morning. By Friday afternoon the \nlinger upper low and boundary near the SE CWA will continue to \nmean some risk for showers and t-storm. Chances will be best \naround the mountains and others will be possible in SE WA and the \nlower ID Panhandle near that boundary and some of the better lapse\nrates and instability. \n\nWind will remain a bit breezy today, especially over central WA \nwith winds from the south to southwest at 10 to 15 mph and gusts \nup to 20 mph. However comparatively lighter winds are forecast for\nFriday. Highs will be in the 60s to lower 70s. Lows will in the \nupper 30s and 40s Thursday night and mid-30s to 40s Friday \nnight/Saturday AM. We will have to watch for some patchy frost \nthen.\n\nSaturday to Wednesday: The Inland NW remains dominated by the\ntrough of low pressure and boundary stalled near the eastern CWA\nfor some of this period. That boundary will give some embedded\ndisturbances something to focus along. That will bring some\nmoderate precipitation chances through the weekend and Monday,\nwith the highest chances over the eastern third of WA and ID.\nModels are not entirely in good agreement on the evolution of the\nprecise waves and precipitation potential, so the forecast will\ncontinue to be fine-tuned. Models trends toward the low losing\ninfluence Tuesday and Wednesday, Some embedded t-storms will\nremain a risk, but the potential is inconsistent as the focus has\nbeen changing day to day. Expect moderating temperatures into the\nweekend. /Solveig",
